WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.267
Last time, taking turns took 54 rounds.

00:00:03.307 --> 00:00:04.868
And we ended on a promise:

00:00:04.908 --> 00:00:09.075
let the sliders and the dial
step together, and it takes five.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.125
But stepping together is
harder than it sounds.

00:00:12.165 --> 00:00:16.947
When Dune's slider moved, two episodes
ago, the dial's meter moved too.

00:00:16.987 --> 00:00:22.329
To step together, every control has to
know how its move disturbs every meter.

00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:28.848
For five clinics, that is a small table. For
7,000 providers, it is 50 million numbers.

00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:31.222
So how can this possibly be fast?

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.021
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:35.061 --> 00:00:37.075
Today: the arrow matrix.

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:42.561
Here is that table for our
five clinics, at the fit.

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:46.316
One row for each meter, one
column for each control.

00:00:46.356 --> 00:00:50.184
Each cell says how much that meter
moves when that control does.

00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:56.297
Down the diagonal is each clinic's
own wobble, from last time:

00:00:56.337 --> 00:01:01.749
6.38 for Alder, 6.66 for
Birch, down to 0.02 for Elm.

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:05.126
In the corner is the dial's own: 10.2.

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:09.664
Along the two edges is how strongly
each clinic is tied to the dial.

00:01:09.704 --> 00:01:16.774
Birch, with 24 higher-risk patients,
most of all: 5.94. Elm, with none: zero.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.733
And everywhere else, nothing.

00:01:18.773 --> 00:01:24.028
Alder's slider cannot move Birch's meter,
because no patient belongs to both.

00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:27.356
That is not an approximation. It is the model.

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:30.093
Look at the shape it leaves: an arrowhead.

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:35.424
With five clinics, the shape
hardly matters. So scale up.

00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:40.056
Our crowd of 200 providers,
with two risk factors,

00:01:40.096 --> 00:01:46.097
has a table of 40,804 cells.
Only 1,004 of them are not zero.

00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:50.622
A national measure, with 7,000
providers and 50 risk factors,

00:01:50.662 --> 00:01:55.153
has almost 50 million cells.
Nearly all of them are empty.

00:01:55.193 --> 00:02:00.748
Treat that as an ordinary table, and you
are stuck. Use the arrow, and it is easy.

00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:06.173
Here is how. Take one clinic at a time.

00:02:06.213 --> 00:02:09.321
Square its tie to the dial,
divide by its own wobble,

00:02:09.361 --> 00:02:11.790
and take that away from the corner.

00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:22.773
Alder takes away 0.15. Birch, 5.30.
Cedar, 1.45. Dune, 0.38. Elm, nothing.

00:02:22.813 --> 00:02:26.704
The corner falls from 10.2 to 2.93.

00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:29.507
And now the sliders are out of the problem.

00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:33.242
What is left is one small
number, for the dial alone.

00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:36.772
That number has a meaning.
It adds up, clinic by clinic,

00:02:36.812 --> 00:02:41.092
how much patients differ in risk
from others in their own clinic.

00:02:41.132 --> 00:02:44.732
Elm, where every patient is
lower-risk, adds nothing.

00:02:44.772 --> 00:02:49.930
It is Episode 4's idea again: the
risk rule is learned inside clinics.

00:02:50.067 --> 00:02:52.515
It is also a warning. Ignore the clinics,

00:02:52.555 --> 00:02:55.913
and the data would seem to
say three and a half times as

00:02:55.953 --> 00:02:58.609
much about the dial as they really do.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.792
So the dial's step takes one division.
From the plain guess, it is 1.81.

00:03:06.832 --> 00:03:11.577
Then each clinic takes its own step,
corrected for what the dial just did.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:18.180
Watch the machine. One joint
step, and the dial reads 1.81.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:23.031
A second: 2.00. A third: 2.0164, the top.

00:03:23.071 --> 00:03:28.178
Five steps until the dial stops
moving, where taking turns needed 54.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:33.364
And the trouble with where we put zero?
Record the risk factor five units higher,

00:03:33.404 --> 00:03:36.778
or fifty. The joint path
does not change at all.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:41.975
Each step is three cheap jobs:
add up over the patients,

00:03:42.015 --> 00:03:45.943
fold once for each provider,
and solve one small system.

00:03:45.983 --> 00:03:50.307
We tried it on 2,000 providers
and about 200,000 patients.

00:03:50.347 --> 00:03:53.181
The fit took well under a second.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.973
Written out as an ordinary table,
with a column for every provider,

00:03:58.013 --> 00:04:02.866
the same data would fill 3.3
gigabytes before any fitting began.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.012
So a separate number for every
provider is not a computing problem.

00:04:07.052 --> 00:04:08.784
It only looked like one.

00:04:08.824 --> 00:04:13.928
One caution. The arrow needs every
patient to belong to exactly one provider.

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:18.326
A second grouping that cuts
across the first would break it.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.396
Let's gather it up.

00:04:21.436 --> 00:04:26.025
Clinics disturb each other only through
the dial, so the table is an arrow.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:30.271
Fold each clinic into the corner,
and one small system is left.

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:36.037
Nothing is approximated. It is last
episode's step, taken for everything at once.

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:39.378
And keep an eye on that folded number, 2.93.

00:04:39.418 --> 00:04:43.466
It says how firmly the data
hold the dial in place.

00:04:43.506 --> 00:04:45.712
Next time: how sure are we?

00:04:45.833 --> 00:04:49.938
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
